    "source": "Changesets CLI documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "The Changesets action updates a version pull request while changesets exist and invokes a configured publish command after that pull request is merged.",
    "evidence": "https://github.com/changesets/action",
    "source": "Changesets action documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Changesets distinguishes fixed groups from linked groups and computes unpublished releases before publishing; its publisher recognizes already-published failures but delegates retry correctness to custom publishers.",
    "evidence": "https://github.com/changesets/changesets/blob/main/docs/linked-packages.md and https://github.com/changesets/changesets/blob/main/packages/cli/src/commands/publish/index.ts#L103-L147 and #L249-L387",
    "source": "Changesets documentation and publish implementation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Knope's multi-package model creates one tag and GitHub Release per package, and its standard release workflow is command-driven rather than a merged release pull request being the native authorization boundary.",
    "evidence": "https://knope.tech/reference/config-file/steps/release/ and https://knope.tech/tutorials/releasing-multiple-packages/ and https://knope.tech/tutorials/releasing-basic-projects/",
    "source": "Knope documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Knope supports release assets by creating a draft release, uploading assets serially, and publishing the release, but treats an existing release as a conflict and provides no documented checksum, attestation, or fresh-runner convergence guarantee.",
    "evidence": "https://knope.tech/reference/config-file/steps/release/#release-assets and https://knope.tech/reference/config-file/steps/release/#errors",
    "source": "Knope release-step documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Nx Release defaults to fixed versioning with one v{version} tag and supports a configurable release tag pattern; independent projects default to projectName@version.",
    "evidence": "https://nx.dev/docs/reference/nx-json#release-tag and https://nx.dev/docs/guides/nx-release/release-groups",
    "source": "Nx Release documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Nx publication is a first-class per-project executor and its npm guidance checks registry state and skips an already-published version, making repeated npm publication idempotent at that boundary.",
    "evidence": "https://nx.dev/docs/guides/nx-release/publish-in-ci-cd and https://nx.dev/docs/kb/release-npm-packages#future-releases and https://github.com/nrwl/nx/blob/master/packages/nx/src/command-line/release/publish.ts#L41-L52",
    "source": "Nx documentation and publish implementation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Nx dry-run reaches publish executors, but its documented preview is not an isolated end-to-end rehearsal with registry, GitHub asset, attestation, interruption, and resume substitutes.",
    "evidence": "https://nx.dev/docs/features/manage-releases and https://github.com/nrwl/nx/blob/master/packages/nx/src/command-line/release/publish.ts#L219-L225",
    "source": "Nx documentation and publish implementation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Release Please creates or updates a release pull request and creates tags and GitHub Releases after merge, while leaving external package publication to a separate tool.",
    "evidence": "https://github.com/googleapis/release-please/blob/main/docs/cli.md and https://github.com/googleapis/release-please/blob/main/docs/manifest-releaser.md",
    "source": "Release Please documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Release Please describes GitHub release creation as non-transactional but safely rerunnable, and documents separate gh release upload steps for assets; that guarantee does not cover registry publication or exact asset verification.",
    "evidence": "https://github.com/googleapis/release-please/blob/main/docs/cli.md#create-a-manifest-release-deprecated and https://github.com/googleapis/release-please-action#attaching-artifacts-to-releases",
    "source": "Release Please CLI and action documentation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Callisto's model contains an ArtifactUpload operation, but the live release planner never constructs it and the prepared-operation executor has no dispatch or observation branch for it.",
    "evidence": "crates/callisto-model/src/release.rs:936; crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release.rs:348-371,976,1054,1090",
    "source": "Callisto origin/main release model and executor inventory",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "Callisto currently derives durable release tags from each package's tag template, so its live implementation cannot produce the required single callisto@X.Y.Z product identity for a fixed train without a canonical product-release concept.",
    "evidence": "crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release.rs:1059-1064 and crates/callisto-model/src/tag.rs:12-116",
    "source": "Callisto origin/main tag derivation",
    "confidence": "confirmed"
  },
  {
    "claim": "None of the four examined tools documents one terminal verification contract spanning registries, an exact remote tag target, a GitHub Release, every binary/WASM asset, checksums, and attestations after interruption.",
    "evidence": "Targeted review of the cited official documentation and source for all four tools found partial idempotency and dry-run behavior but no cross-provider convergence contract.",
    "source": "Cross-tool comparison",
    "confidence": "likely"
  }
]

"The live ReleaseOperationId and ArtifactSlotId force package ownership, so adding a workflow matrix alone would preserve the wrong identity and leave uploads unreachable. Registry package bytes are included in the artifact handoff because exact already-present verification cannot be defined from package/version alone."
[
  "Independent package versioning or per-package GitHub tags and releases before version 1.0.",
  "Windows, Intel macOS, Linux glibc, or WASI targets other than wasm32-wasip1.",
  "Apple Developer ID signing, notarization, or use of a maintainer's personal signing identity.",
  "Provider observation, interruption recovery, or workflow job layout, which are specified separately.",
  "Backward compatibility for the current unreleased package-owned Tag, ForgeRelease, ArtifactSlotId, or release-intent JSON shapes."
]
"Supersedes the package-owned tag/forge and unreachable binary-slot assumptions in the draft .claude release specifications. The repository is pre-v1 and has no compatibility requirement for those unreleased shapes."

